{
"page": 29,
"rows": [
[
"Revolving Credit Facilities: Fossil Firms’ Corporate Credit Card of Choice\nA revolving credit facility (RCF) is a line of credit that a company can draw down, repay, and redraw repeatedly up"
],
[
"to an agreed limit.27 Our database credits banks with the full amount of the RCF each time they make a new credit"
],
[
"agreement or renew or modify terms of an existing credit agreement. Our database only counts the commitment\namount of the RCF, not the cumulative amount drawn by the client. Fossil fuel companies may draw on these\nagreements multiple times or not at all.\nThe BOCC database illustrates just how pervasively fossil fuel firms use this particular type of credit. In BOCC, RCFs\nare second only to bond issuances for the top financing type, and make up 39.7% of the total adjusted league\ncredit, as shown above. Some banks argue that RCFs only represent commitments to finance but not necessarily\nactual dispersals of funds, and thus shouldn’t be included in fossil fuel financing league tables. RCFs are a\ncritical part of the fossil fuel financing stack.28 They provide critical liquidity, are an ongoing barometer of bank\nconfidence and borrower creditworthiness,29 and provide the type of operational flexibility especially suited to\ncompanies reliant on volatile cash flows like fossil fuel firms. Each new RCF is a renewed bank commitment which\nupholds the creditworthiness of the fossil client. Ultimately, finance is as much about creditworthiness and access\nto capital as it is about specific outlays, and RCFs provide a critical lifeline to both. What’s more, many bank\nregulators treat RCFs as lower risk than drawn debt, applying credit conversion factors that reduce the regulatory\ncapital required.30 RCFs’ role in the climate crisis therefore deserves more — not less — attention from financial\nregulators."
]
]
},
{
"page": 31,
"rows": [
[
"FOSSIL FUEL EXPANSION FINANCE",
null
],
[
null,
""
],
[
null,
"In 2025, bank financiers provided $508 billion to companies expanding fossil fuel developments — up a full $108 billion since\n2024. That’s a 27.1% hike in one year to support an extension and potential lock-in of decades of future climate emissions and\nenergy instability. In 2025, top banks increased expansion financing across the oil and gas value chain, especially in the midstream\nand power sectors. In another regressive trend, financiers also increased lending and underwriting to coal power and coal mining\nexpansion companies.\nGeographically, much of the financing dedicated to oil and gas companies expanding fossil fuel projects occurs in the United\nStates. Chinese coal companies, at least for now, receive the grand majority of new coal expansion financing. This growth in fossil\nfuel expansion finance is troubling because new developments lock in decades of fossil fuel dependence and climate emissions.37"
]
]
},
